\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_daf_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task death rate differential vs.\ performance gap ($n{=}11$ task types). The near-perfect correlation ($r = 0.97$) is a structural consequence of death dominance (DAF $\to 1 \Rightarrow r \to 1$), not an independent finding. The sole outlier (\texttt{find-non-living-thing}, red diamond) has zero deaths; its $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +9.58$ arises entirely from non-death score differences.}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

We analyze 11 of ScienceWorld's 30 task types (245 episodes); the 19 excluded types lack actionable routing signal due to development contamination (5), taxonomic redundancy (7), or agent-floor performance (7). An anti-cherry-picking analysis confirms death dominance holds in 18 of these 19 types ($\overline{\Delta\text{DR}} = +38.2$\,pp $>$ included tasks' $+28.7$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:per-task}).

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\scriptsize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2.5pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrccc@{}}
\toprule
Task & $n$ & $\Delta_\text{full}$ & D\%\textsubscript{int} & D\%\textsubscript{det} & D / I / N \\
\midrule
id-life-stages-1       & 20  & $+47.50$ & 55   & 10   & 9 / 0 / 11  \\
lifespan-long          & 25  & $+44.00$ & 56   & 16   & 11 / 1 / 13 \\
lifespan-l-then-s      & 20  & $+40.80$ & 45   & 10   & 8 / 1 / 11  \\
inclined-plane         & 20  & $+36.00$ & 55   & 20   & 7 / 0 / 13  \\
freeze                 & 20  & $+30.50$ & 55   & 25   & 6 / 0 / 14  \\
meas-melt-point        & 20  & $+30.05$ & 55   & 25   & 6 / 0 / 14  \\
id-life-stages-2       & 20  & $+30.00$ & 50   & 20   & 6 / 0 / 14  \\
find-animal            & 20  & $+26.65$ & 50   & 25   & 5 / 0 / 15  \\
grow-fruit             & 20  & $+25.10$ & 45   & 20   & 6 / 1 / 13  \\
chem-mix               & 20  & $+21.80$ & 45   & 25   & 6 / 2 / 12  \\
\midrule
find-non-living$^\dagger$ & 40 & $+9.58$ & 0  & 0    & 16 / 4 / 20 \\
\midrule
\textbf{All (11 tasks)} & \textbf{245} & $\mathbf{+29.60}$ & \textbf{42.9} & \textbf{16.3} & \textbf{86 / 9 / 150} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Per-task decomposition. D\%: death rate for internal/deterministic. D/I/N: det-preferred / int-preferred / no-preference labels. $\dagger$\,Only task with zero deaths; $n{=}40$ (two batches).}
\label{tab:per-task}
\end{table}

Of the 95 non-tie routing labels (Figure~\ref{fig:label-decomposition}), 75 (78.9\%; 95\% CI: $[70.7\%, 87.0\%]$, episode-level bootstrap) arise from death asymmetry: the internal strategy triggers a catastrophic $-100$ penalty while the deterministic strategy survives. This penalty-independent label fraction is the primary diagnostic metric. Of these, 54 are sole-signal labels where death was the only discriminator; at $P{=}0$ these reduce to ties, but the labeling function itself is penalty-independent (Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling}).

DAF $= 90.4\%$ at $P{=}100$ ($\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$, $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.84$; 95\% CI$_{\text{DAF}}$: $[81.3\%, 99.1\%]$, episode-level bootstrap). The 78.9\% label fraction provides the more robust, penalty-invariant characterization (\S\ref{sec:daf}).

\paragraph{Per-task structure.}
The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes versus 16.3\% for deterministic; all 105 internal deaths are triggered by the \texttt{focus on} action (Appendix~\ref{app:failure-modes}). Per-task $\Delta$DR correlates with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ at $r = 0.97$ (Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}), a structural consequence of DAF$\to 1$ rather than an independent finding. Figure~\ref{fig:death-timeline} illustrates the typical death trajectory.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/death_action_timeline.pdf}
\vspace{-2mm}
\caption{Action sequences for 6 representative death episodes. The fatal \texttt{focus on} (arrow) appears in final steps after long \texttt{connect} sequences (``connect loop $\to$ fatal focus'').}
\label{fig:death-timeline}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Binary structure and robustness.}
All 10 death-prone task types produce 100\% death-asymmetry labels; only \texttt{find-non-living-thing} (zero deaths) produces non-death signal (SCI $= 0.21$; Appendix~\ref{app:sci}). Leave-one-task-out analysis (Table~\ref{tab:loto-daf}) confirms robustness: removing any single death-prone task changes DAF by at most 3.0~pp. Bootstrap 95\% CIs: DAF $[81.3\%, 99.1\%]$; cluster-robust $[73.3\%, 100.0\%]$ ($K{=}11$; Appendix~\ref{app:wild-cluster}). Two independent replications (Gemma-2-9B-it, DAF${=}89.7\%$; seed=123, DAF${=}89.7\%$) converge within 0.7~pp.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\scriptsize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}
\toprule
Removed Task & $N_\text{rem}$ & DAF (\%) & $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
(none, full) & 245 & 78.9 & --- \\
\midrule
Find Non-Living$^\dagger$ & 205 & 100.0 & +21.1 \\
Lifespan Longest & 220 & 75.9 & $-$3.0 \\
Life Stages 1 & 225 & 76.7 & $-$2.2 \\
Lifespan Long+Short & 225 & 76.7 & $-$2.2 \\
Chemistry Mix & 225 & 77.0 & $-$1.9 \\
Grow Fruit & 225 & 77.3 & $-$1.7 \\
Inclined Plane & 225 & 77.3 & $-$1.7 \\
Freeze & 225 & 77.5 & $-$1.4 \\
Life Stages 2 & 225 & 77.5 & $-$1.4 \\
Melt Pt Unknown & 225 & 77.5 & $-$1.4 \\
Find Animal & 225 & 77.8 & $-$1.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Leave-one-task-out DAF sensitivity (label fraction). Removing any death-prone task changes DAF by $\leq$3.0\,pp. $\dagger$\,Sole death-free task; removal raises DAF to 100\% by construction.}
\label{tab:loto-daf}
\end{table}

\subsection{Multi-Model Validation (7--9B Tier)}
\label{sec:cross-model}

Llama-3.1-8B~\citep{llama3-2024} ($N{=}220$) and Gemma-2-9B-it~\citep{gemma2-2024} ($N{=}220$) independently replicate death dominance: DAF $= 88.2\%$ and $89.7\%$ respectively, with direction consistency 10/10 on death-prone tasks (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model}). Three architecturally distinct model families---Qwen (Alibaba), Llama (Meta), Gemma (Google)---converge on DAF $88$--$90\%$ with a spread of only 2.2~pp, despite differences in pretraining data, architecture, and tokenization. This narrow convergence suggests that death dominance is an environment-level property rather than a model-specific artifact. Scaling $10{\times}$ to two 70B-class models (\S\ref{sec:agent-improvement}) intensifies rather than attenuates the pattern (DAF${=}92.4$--$101.0\%$), contrary to the intuition that larger models would reduce the failure gap.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\scriptsize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2pt}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcrrrcl@{}}
\toprule
Model & Env & $n$ & Int DR & Det DR & $\Delta$DR & DAF [95\% CI] & Signal \\
\midrule
Qwen2.5-7B & SW & 245 & 42.9 & 16.3 & +26.5 & 90.4 [81.3, 99.1] & death \\
Llama-3.1-8B & SW & 220 & 41.4 & 11.4 & +30.0 & 88.2 [80.2, 95.1] & death \\
Gemma-2-9B & SW & 220 & 36.4 & 14.1 & +22.3 & 89.7 [79.5, 98.4] & death \\
Qwen2.5-72B & SW & 255 & 30.6 & 2.8 & +27.8 & 92.4 [86.1, 97.9] & death \\
Llama-70B & SW & 220 & 48.6 & 20.0 & +28.6 & 101.0 [95.3, 107.5] & death \\
\midrule
Qwen2.5-7B & ALF & 135 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & info-asym \\
Llama-3.1-8B & ALF & 135 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & info-asym \\
\midrule
Qwen2.5-7B & MATH & 795 & --- & --- & --- & --- & none \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Cross-model and cross-environment summary. SW = ScienceWorld, ALF = ALFWorld. Death dominance replicates across three 7--9B families and intensifies under $10{\times}$ scaling; ALFWorld shows information-asymmetry signal; MATH shows none. DR = death rate (\%).}
\label{tab:cross-model-env}
\end{table}

\subsection{Agent Improvement Baselines}
\label{sec:agent-improvement}

We test whether agent-level improvement reduces death dominance (Table~\ref{tab:agent-improvement}). Retry ($N{=}5$) yields marginal reduction ($43.5\%$ vs.\ $48.0\%$). Reflexion~\citep{reflexion-2023} reduces death to $31.4\%$ but the gap to deterministic remains $15.1$~pp; under matched-episode analysis ($N{=}3$), $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = -0.47$, yielding DAF${}>100\%$. Scaling to Qwen2.5-72B ($N{=}255$) reduces death rate to $30.6\%$ with DAF $= 92.4\%$ (95\% CI: $[86.1\%, 97.9\%]$). A second 70B model, Llama-3.1-70B ($N{=}220$), yields DAF $= 101.0\%$ ($[95.3\%, 107.5\%]$): death avoidance more than fully accounts for the gap. Two 70B-class models confirm that scaling intensifies rather than attenuates death dominance.

\paragraph{Dose-response analysis.}
We extend both interventions to $N{=}5$ trials on death-prone tasks ($n{=}200$; Figure~\ref{fig:dose-response}). Retry is nearly flat ($48.0\% \to 43.5\%$). Reflexion drops steeply to $34.0\%$ at $N{=}3$ ($\Delta = -13.5$~pp, $p = 0.003$) then stabilizes ($34$--$36.5\%$ for $N \geq 3$, pairwise $p > 0.5$); both the decline and plateau are well-powered (MDE ${\sim}8$~pp at 80\% power; Appendix~\ref{app:dose-response}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Death rate vs.\ trial count for Retry and Reflexion on 10 death-prone tasks ($n{=}200$). Reflexion drops steeply through $N{=}3$ then plateaus; Retry is nearly flat. Horizontal lines: 72B ($30.6\%$) and deterministic ($16.3\%$) baselines. Error bands: Wilson 95\% CIs.}
\label{fig:dose-response}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Environment Boundary Probes}
\label{sec:math-contrast}
\label{sec:cross-env}

We probe two environments as boundary cases to test the framework's specificity (Figure~\ref{fig:signal-reversal}). On MATH Level~4--5~\citep{math-2021} ($N{=}795$), internal reasoning and code-interpreter accuracy are near-identical ($73.5\%$ vs.\ $73.0\%$, $p = 0.81$): without a catastrophic failure mechanism, no routing signal emerges despite substantial task difficulty. ALFWorld~\citep{alfworld-2021} (135 games, no death mechanism, DAF $= 0\%$) shows 94.6\% of non-tie labels favoring the information-advantaged strategy (which enumerates admissible actions); Llama-3.1-8B replicates at 100\%. The framework diagnoses this qualitatively different mechanism---information asymmetry rather than death avoidance---without modification, confirming that DAF correctly attributes zero signal to death when none exists.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/signal_reversal.pdf}
\vspace{-2mm}
\caption{Cross-environment label distributions. ScienceWorld and ALFWorld both favor deterministic via different mechanisms (death avoidance vs.\ information asymmetry); MATH shows no routing signal.}
\label{fig:signal-reversal}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}). Random routing ($-22.38$) falls between the two pure strategies, confirming that the gap is not an artifact of evaluation methodology. The oracle improves only to $-6.58$, with 96.3\% of decisions matching always-deterministic---an \emph{oracle degeneracy} where perfect per-episode foresight provides marginal benefit over the trivial always-deterministic baseline (Appendix~\ref{sec:threshold-routing}).

\subsection{Router Training: Inconclusive Results}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

A Qwen3 LoRA router (0.6B--4B; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}) trained on counterfactual labels shows flat step-level balanced accuracy ($56$--$59\%$) across all model scales; episode-level evaluation yields $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$, below majority-vote.\footnote{Consistent with noisy-label bounds~\citep{natarajan2013learning}: ${\sim}$88\% of step-level labels are uninformative with 90.5\%/3.7\% class imbalance (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}).} Leave-one-task-out degrades to chance ($49.2\%$), confirming signal is a per-task-type constant rather than a learnable function of step-level observations. Deployed end-to-end, the best router (4B) scores $-10.70$, not exceeding always-deterministic ($-9.47$); this negative result is consistent with the death-dominance finding, as death avoidance cannot be predicted from pre-episode features when it depends on multi-step trajectory dynamics.

